course: dictionary
lesson: 15520
title: Big and solid: substantial
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Substantial» is a useful word for big, important things. It can mean large in amount or size, as in «The school made a substantial donation to the library.» A donation that is substantial is a big gift, not a tiny one.
explain: The word can also mean strong, solid and well made. «The shelf was made of substantial oak, so it did not wobble.» Here the wood is thick and sturdy. Another meaning is about food: «We had a substantial breakfast of eggs, toast and porridge.» A substantial meal is big and filling.
explain: Use the clues around the word to check. In «There was a substantial increase in the number of visitors», the word increase tells you numbers went up, and substantial tells you by a lot. Ask yourself: is it big? If you swap in «big» and the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
fact: Substantial means large in amount, size or importance.
fact: Substantial can also mean strong, solid and well made.
fact: For food, substantial means big and filling, as in «a substantial meal».
fact: Common phrases with substantial: «a substantial amount», «a substantial increase», «a substantial difference».
fact: Substantial is a common word in formal writing and exams, so it is worth learning well.
exercise: «We ate a substantial breakfast before the match.» What does substantial mean here? | Tiny and light | Cold and wet | *Big and filling | Late in the day
exercise: «The shelf was made of substantial oak, so it did not bend.» What does substantial suggest about the wood? | *Strong and solid | Thin and bendy | Painted bright red
exercise: «There was a substantial increase in visitors this year.» Which word in the sentence tells you numbers went up? | visitors | substantial | *increase | this
test: Which phrase means a big rise in numbers? | a quick look | a faint glow | a tiny whisper | *a substantial increase
test: A teacher says the class made a substantial difference to the test scores. What does this mean? | A difference that is hard to see | *A big, important difference | A difference in colour
exam: Which word means large in amount or size? | *substantial | tiny | quick | dim
